===Historic vehicles===
[[File:Unidentified Car, Mill St. Stirling, Ontario 1776 (14191918725) (cropped).jpg|thumbnail|Example of an Ontario Historic plate, affixed to the front of a vehicle.]]
Historic vehicle licence plates were introduced in 1969 as yearly plates. In 1973, plates switched to permanently issued plates validated with stickers.

Vehicles more than 30 years old and substantially unchanged since manufacture may qualify for a "Historic" registration.<ref>{{cite web|title=Highway Traffic Act, R.S.O. 1990, CHAPTER H.8, s7|url=https://www.ontario.ca/laws/statute/90h08#BK15|website=eLaws|date=24 July 2014 |publisher=Queen's Printer for Ontario|access-date=17 September 2015}}</ref>

Annual fees were much lower ($18 vs $120 for a passenger car until 2022),<ref name=mtofees>{{cite web|title=Fees for other types of vehicles|url=https://www.ontario.ca/driving-and-roads/register-vehicle-permit-licence-plate-and-sticker#fees|website=Ontario Ministry of Transportation|publisher=Queen's Printer for Ontario|access-date=17 September 2015}}</ref> but historic vehicles may not be used as conventional transportation. They legally may only be driven to and from events and parades where the vehicle is on display, to garages for maintenance, and other similar use cases. Annual renewal stickers are affixed to the rear plate, as with passenger vehicles.

Historic plates are not to be confused with year-of-manufacture plates.<ref>{{cite web|title=Vehicles must be 30 years old for historic plate designation  |url=http://www.wheels.ca/news/vehicles-must-be-30-years-old-for-historic-plate-designation/|website=wheels.ca|date=23 August 2008 |publisher=Toronto Star Newspapers and Metroland news|access-date=17 September 2015}}</ref>

