==Manufacturing==
[[File:Ontario avery 9903.png|thumbnail|Since 1994, Ontario plates have had a reflective backing. Between 1999 and 2003, the backing was made by [[Avery Dennison|Avery]]. These can be recognized by the slogan written with smaller letters, and the presence of a registration mark below the bottom left bolt hole. Backing has since been made using [[3M]]-brand reflective material.|173x173px]]
Ontario licence plates were formerly manufactured by prison inmates at the Millbrook Correctional Centre in [[Cavan-Millbrook-North Monaghan]].<ref>{{cite book |last=Sallmen |first=Joseph |date=2003 |title=Ontario License Plates: A Century of History |page=66 |publisher=J.P. Sallmen |isbn=0973414405 }}</ref>  Upon Millbrook's closure in 2003,<ref>{{cite news|last=Graham|first=Karen|date=2015|title=End of an Era as the Jail Comes Tumbling Down|url=http://themillbrooktimes.ca/end-of-an-era-as-the-jail-comes-tumbling-down/|location=Millbrook, Ontario|access-date=2017-10-11}}</ref> manufacture was moved to the [[Central East Correctional Centre]] in Lindsay, Ontario.<ref>{{cite web|title=First look at where Ontario inmates make blankets, licence plates|url=https://www.ctvnews.ca/london/article/first-look-at-where-ontario-inmates-make-blankets-licence-plates/|website=CTV News London|date=17 March 2015 |publisher=CTV Television Network|access-date=24 August 2015}}</ref>  Since 1991, all Ontario plates have been manufactured for the MTO by Trilcor Industries, owned by the province's [[Ministry of the Solicitor General (Ontario)|Ministry of the Solicitor General]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Treatment Programs;Trilcor Industries|url=http://www.mcscs.jus.gov.on.ca/english/corr_serv/adult_off/OffenderProgramsServices/treat_prog_trilcor/treat_prog_trilcor.html|website=Ministry of Community Safety and Correctional Services|publisher=Queen's Printer for Ontario|access-date=24 August 2015|archive-date=15 September 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150915085521/http://www.mcscs.jus.gov.on.ca/english/corr_serv/adult_off/OffenderProgramsServices/treat_prog_trilcor/treat_prog_trilcor.html|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>[http://www.mcscs.jus.gov.on.ca/english/corr_serv/adult_off/treat_prog/treat_prog_trilcor/treat_prog_trilcor.html Government of Ontario, Canada / Gouvernement de l'Ontario, Canada] {{in lang|en|fr}} {{webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110717173317/http://www.mcscs.jus.gov.on.ca/english/corr_serv/adult_off/treat_prog/treat_prog_trilcor/treat_prog_trilcor.html |date=July 17, 2011 }}</ref> From 2017 to 2018, the plates were manufactured by Waldale Manufacturing of Nova Scotia, because Ontario's prison made plates ran out due to the peeling and bubbling in the preceding "B" series. The plates made by Trilcor Industries returned in 2018, to avoid license plate issues with the [[Vehicle registration plates of Nova Scotia|Nova Scotia license plates]].

===Alternative supplier===
In 2016, an increase in the rate of defective plates combined with an increase in the number of registered vehicles led to the Ontario Ministry of Transportation becoming unable to keep up with demand for plates. It placed an order for 100,000 units from the Waldale Irwin Holdson Group, the largest licence plate manufacturer in North America.<ref>{{cite web|title=About us|url=http://www.waldale.com/about-us.php|website=Waldale Irwin Hodson Group|access-date=18 January 2017|archive-date=31 January 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170131184212/http://www.waldale.com/about-us.php|url-status=dead}}</ref> Plates are produced by the Waldale Manufacturing facility in [[Amherst, Nova Scotia]].

Plates from the first batch of 35,000 can be identified due to the use of embossed letters and numbers from [[Vehicle registration plates of Nova Scotia|Nova Scotia plates]], which differ in appearance from Ontarian fonts.<ref>{{cite web|title=Ontario special orders licence plates to meet high demand|url=http://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/windsor/licence-plates-ontario-nova-scotia-1.3939234|website=CBC News|publisher=Canadian Broadcasting Corporation|access-date=18 January 2017}}</ref> The province continues to order supplementary batches of plates from Waldale. Since 2017, the Ontario die set has been in use. These plates are almost identical to those made by Trilcor Industries. The Waldale made plates have squared off edges and smaller bolt holes.<ref>{{cite web |title=Ontario Project |url=http://www.andrewturnbull.net/plates/ontario-p1.html#passenger |website=The Andrew Turnbull Network |access-date=10 October 2022}}</ref>

== Issues and controversies ==

===Defective plates from 2012===
In 2012, reports began to appear of plates deteriorating earlier than otherwise expected. The reflective layers detached themselves from the metal plate, making the plate unreadable. Approximately 1% of licence plates issued have this defect. The defect has appeared in both front and rear plates. Trilcor Industries and the MTO offer a five-year warranty on plates and will replace the defective plates at no cost.<ref name="PeelingRecord">{{cite news|last1=Warren|first1=May|date=22 January 2015|title=Peeling licence plates a problem across Ontario|work=Waterloo Region Record|publisher=Metroland News|url=http://www.therecord.com/news-story/5271532-peeling-licence-plates-a-problem-across-ontario/|access-date=2 September 2015}}</ref> Plate replacement for other reasons (theft, damage, wear, etc.) is done at a cost.

Conventional plates can be replaced "over-the-counter" at a licence office, but the complete process for personalized plates takes over six months.<ref name="ServiceOntReplace">{{cite web|title=How can I replace a lost, stolen, or damaged licence plate or personalized licence plate?|url=https://www.ontario.ca/faq/how-can-i-replace-lost-stolen-or-damaged-licence-plate-or-personalized-licence-plate|access-date=2 September 2015|website=Service Ontario|publisher=Queen's Printer for Ontario}}</ref>

Driving with an illegible plate is an offence punishable by fine, under the Highway Traffic Act.<ref name="HTAs12">{{cite web|title=Highway Traffic Act (Highway Traffic Act, R.S.O. 1990, c. H.8)|url=https://www.ontario.ca/laws/statute/90h08#BK37|access-date=2 September 2015|website=ontario.ca|date=24 July 2014 |publisher=Queen's Printer for Ontario}}</ref>

In 2015, the Ministry of Community Safety and Correctional Services hired [[National Research Council (Canada)|Canada's National Research Council]] to identify the root cause of licence plate de-lamination. The NRC's report indicated that the reflective material adhered poorly to the aluminium plates, and that embossing process stressed the materials to the point that the reflective layer would puncture and de-laminate. At that point, water and other contaminants could slip between the layers. Also, road de-icing materials contributed to the de-lamination. The report found that this problem was present in samples from all types of plates, except for motorcycle plates.

The report recommended that Trilcor work with its supplier of laminating layer to resolve the problem, and indicated that a thicker layer of material would likely perform better.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Howorun|first1=Christina|date=22 September 2016|title=Thousands of Ontario licence plates deemed defective|publisher=Rogers Digital Media|agency=City News|url=https://toronto.citynews.ca/2016/09/19/thousands-of-ontario-licence-plates-deemed-defective/|access-date=18 January 2017}}</ref>

